{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they shall send out of the camp every leper, etc.</b> After he arranged all three camps, each man by his standard, therefore he immediately mentioned the order of sanctity of these three camps: for the leper is sent out of all three, and the zav outside two, and one impure by reason of a corpse outside the camp of the Shekhinah. And this order of sending away accords with the words of the midrash (Naso 7:10) which says: 'And they shall send out of the camp every leper' — this is idolatry, etc.; 'and every zav' — this is forbidden sexual relations, for he comes from an emission of seed; 'and everyone impure by a corpse' — this is bloodshed, etc.; and he interpreted this whole verse concerning the exiles. And the question is difficult: who even mentions the exiles by name? Is it really the intent of the author of this midrash to remove this verse from its plain sense entirely?" "And it appears that the intent of the author of the midrash is to resolve the juxtaposition of this passage here. For it was stated on the first of the month of Nisan, for if not, why did the impure men come to Moses on the fourteenth of Nisan and say 'We are impure by reason of a corpse; why should we be deprived?' etc. (Numbers 9:7) — and if so, why was this passage fixed here? Rather, surely after he set the Tabernacle in its place and caused His Shekhinah, blessed be He, to dwell among them, he came to warn them lest they cause the removal of the Shekhinah by means of these three principal transgressions that cause destruction. Therefore he commanded to send out of the camp every leper and every zav and everyone impure by a corpse, for these three are infallible signs that within them there clings a trace of heresy, of idolatry — for on account of this came the tzara'at, as it says concerning the calf (Exodus 32:25): 'And Moses saw the people, that they were unruly (פָרֻעַ),' and the Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Bamidbar Rabbah 7:4) that they became leprous, as it is written concerning the metzora: 'His head shall be unkempt (פָרוּעַ)' (Leviticus 13:45). And on account of this sin the First and Second Temples were destroyed, for even though they did not worship idolatry in the Second Temple, nevertheless there was among them the sin of baseless hatred and lashon ha-ra, which is weighed as equal to idolatry, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, taught (Yalkut Shimoni on Psalms 636) from 'Who said, With our tongue we will prevail; our lips are our own; who is lord over us?' (Psalms 12:5) — that the speaker of lashon ha-ra is accounted as one who denies the fundamental principle. And the reason for sending the metzora outside the three camps is to distance any trace of idolatry from all three of them. And if it is on account of the sin of lashon ha-ra, his dwelling must be alone, just as he separated between a man and his fellow; and if he is not distanced from all three camps, his dwelling will not be alone. 'And every zav' — for his flux is a sign that he is breach-ridden in forbidden sexual relations, and therefore he comes to suffer a flux. And his removal is from the camp of the holy Shekhinah and from the camp of Leviyah, which also possesses holiness, for every place of holiness cannot tolerate any sexual transgression — as distinguished from the camp of Israel, which does not have so much holiness. 'And everyone impure by a corpse' — for this too indicates a perverse spirit that is within him, and that he is not careful to avoid becoming impure through a human corpse resulting from bloodshed, which touches the image of Elohim, as it were; therefore it is fitting that he be distanced from the camp of the Shekhinah, for he diminishes the honor of the Shekhinah." "And since these three brought about the destruction and the removal of the Shekhinah, therefore he commanded to distance them from the camp, that they not defile their camps, for in a place where they are present, the Holy One, blessed be He, distances His Shekhinah from there, for the place shall be called 'impure, impure.' This is alluded to by the letter ט of טָמֵא — an allusion to bloodshed, for the child is formed over nine months; and for this reason there were six cities of refuge, and it is written (Deuteronomy 19:9): 'And you shall add for yourself yet three cities,' making nine in all and no more, for the reason explained. The letter מ of טָמֵא — an allusion to forbidden sexual relations, for indeed the waters of the Flood were forty days, because they forced their Maker to form the shape of mamzerim during the forty days of the formation of the fetus. The letter א of טָמֵא — an allusion to idolatry, which touches the unity of YHWH, blessed be He, as alluded to in the letter אָלֶף. And therefore King David called the evil impulse טָמֵא (Sukkah 52b), because it caused him to stumble in all three: forbidden sexual relations with Bathsheba, bloodshed with Uriah the Hittite, and idolatry, as they taught (in Sanhedrin 107a) from the verse 'And David came as far as the summit' (II Samuel 15:32) — he sought to worship idolatry, etc. And they also contain impurity, for idolatry defiles like a menstruant, as it is said (Isaiah 30:22): 'You shall cast them away like a menstruous cloth.' Therefore he alluded, in the distancing of these three impure persons, to the distancing of these three principal transgressions that cause the removal of the Shekhinah. And he juxtaposed to this passage 'A man or a woman, when they commit any of the sins of man' — to teach that the distancing of the impure persons is also on account of their sin, as has been said."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:59.077297025Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}